    Task 3: Security Principle Mapping (README)
RSA

RSA provides Confidentiality by encrypting messages so that only the intended receiver can decrypt them using the private key. It also supports Authentication and Non-Repudiation through digital signatures, allowing the sender's identity to be verified.

Diffie-Hellman

Diffie-Hellman provides Secure Key Exchange by allowing two parties to generate the same secret key over an insecure network. It is not an encryption algorithm because it only establishes a shared secret; encryption must be performed using another algorithm such as AES.

Task 4: Threat Model Write-up (README)
(a) Firewall Placement

The firewall should be placed between the Internet and the CampusConnect application servers. It filters incoming traffic before requests reach the servers.

Rule Example

Allow only HTTPS (TCP Port 443)
Block all unnecessary incoming ports
Allow SSH (Port 22) only for administrator IP addresses
(b) IDS Recommendation

CampusConnect should deploy both Host-based IDS (HIDS) and Network-based IDS (NIDS).

HIDS monitors activities occurring on the server itself, including unauthorized file modifications and suspicious logins.

NIDS monitors traffic flowing across the network and detects attacks such as port scanning and denial-of-service attempts before they reach the servers.

(c) HTTP vs HTTPS

CampusConnect should always use HTTPS instead of HTTP.

HTTPS protects users against Man-in-the-Middle (MITM) attacks by encrypting communication between the client and server using TLS. It also prevents session hijacking and credential theft.

(d) Authentication Design

CampusConnect should implement Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).

Two authentication factors:

Password
OTP sent to registered mobile or email

Role permissions:

Student

View assignments
Submit assignments
View grades

Instructor

Create assignments
Grade students
Manage courses

Administrator

Manage users
Manage courses
Configure the system

This follows the Principle of Least Privilege, ensuring each user receives only the permissions required for their role.

(e) Attack Example
SQL Injection (Active Attack)

An attacker enters malicious SQL commands into the login form to bypass authentication or retrieve confidential database information.

This is classified as an Active Attack because the attacker attempts to modify the system's behavior and manipulate database queries.

Conclusion

RSA provides secure encryption and digital signatures, ensuring confidentiality, authentication, and non-repudiation. Diffie-Hellman securely exchanges cryptographic keys over insecure networks. By deploying HTTPS, firewalls, IDS solutions, and multi-factor authentication while following the principle of least privilege, CampusConnect can protect its users and systems from common cyber threats.

